title The row that proved itself twice
prompt The baton, Pheromone seat -- pick up the running thread and take the next agent-doable lap.
rota lap 4512, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. The third seat is what named this lap's one real judgment: the parked ITINERARY would have removed a peer's live account, which is a breach-depth cut taken without a word, so the four pins were re-derived by hand instead of applied.
think The card named stash@{2} as the next lap. git stash list had shifted under that name -- my own fleet_round_open had pushed a new entry eight minutes earlier -- so I read the box by contents rather than by index. git stash show --name-only over the top five found the self_matching_assert family at three depths: stash@{0}, {3}, {4}.
think Three laps proved this package and none of the three sent. The newest, stash@{0}, held the whole thing: both fixtures, the witness, the roster stanza, the REDS row, two recovered records, and a record of its own at 20260910.022718 reading status GREEN.
think That is the row proving itself a second time. %684's other half reads that a lap ending before its commit leaves proof indistinguishable from none, and the lap that wrote that sentence ended before its commit.
think Restored with git checkout stash@{0} -- <paths>, a tree read, so 100755 survived on both fixtures where a blob read lands 100644 (the exec-bit law).
think Re-proven on metal rather than read off the parked log, which is the whole discipline the last two Pheromone laps taught in both directions.
obs The card is the one file a stash cannot carry. The parked ITINERARY was written against a base three commits back; applying it whole would have deleted GRASS's live account and left a dangling half-sentence where the parked block ended. Four pins re-derived by hand against HEAD ff650a8dad. A stash checkout is safe for a file no peer has touched and never for the live front.
obs Fetch-before-book: git fetch xy, then reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 684 -- the parked booking still free after a day in the box. The key is the stamp, 20260909.084248, which the row carries in its own head.
obs REDS reads 40,949 of 40,960, eleven bytes clear, every row OPEN. No fold was taken to make room; the row was already tightened by the lap that drafted it.
obs Two recovered records land on the CLOSED 20260909 shelf, moving it 105 rows to 107; both rosters were corrected off the shelf's own rows by the parked lap and I carried that reading forward unchanged. Whether a closed shelf may take a row for a log recovered after its day stays Keaton's.
